{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nOutbound sync can only write to one tracker project, and it fails closed on anything else. Both\ncreate transports take their project from a single value set once at construction — Cloud\n`AcliClient.create_issue` uses `self.jira_project`\n(`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/acli.py:274-330`) and the Data Center adapter\n`_IssuesMixin.create_issue` uses `self.project`\n(`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira_datacenter/_issues.py:163-189`,\n`fields.setdefault(\"project\", {\"key\": self.project})`). The applier scans every outbound mutation\npre-flight and raises `CrossProjectTargetError` — aborting the whole pass — if any update or delete\ntargets a key outside the one configured project (`applier.py:230-255`, `_cross_project_targets`,\nadded by bug `626d`), and its sole caller (`applier.py:593-610`) feeds it a single string\n`select_backend(load_config()).project`. With a mapping that lists several projects, correct\nmutations to the other projects would be treated as the misconfiguration that guard exists to catch.\n\n## Approach\n\nThree changes: resolve the create target per ticket (covering BOTH transports), relax the guard to\nset membership, and change the guard's caller to supply the set.\n\n**Create targets the ticket's resolved project — resolved once, above both transports.** The\nresolution happens where the create mutation is BUILT, in `_compute_outbound_create_mutation`\n(`outbound_differ.py:502`), which already receives the full local `ticket` (so it can read\n`bridge_project`). It calls `resolve_project(ticket, mapping)` (`projects_store.py:97-110`) against\nthe store mapping (loaded once via `projects_store.read_projects(repo_root)` and threaded into\n`compute_outbound_mutations`). Three outcomes:\n  * resolves to \"not synced\" (absent→no legacy_default, or explicit `\"\"`) → NO create mutation;\n  * resolves to a key **NOT in `mapping.projects`** → NO create mutation (a stale/typo binding must\n    not create against an unsynced project — this closes the create-side gap the guard cannot,\n    since creates are guard-exempt: their key is a local-id placeholder, so `_JIRA_KEY_RE` never\n    matches);\n  * resolves to a valid in-set key → stamped into the mutation's fields under the reserved key\n    `_bridge_target_project`.\nBecause the mutation is consumed by whichever `TicketTransport` is active\n(`dispatch_one.create_one` splats `mutation[\"fields\"]` into `client.create_issue(ticket_data)`\nunchanged), resolving here covers Cloud AND Data Center from one site. Each transport reads\n`ticket_data.get(\"_bridge_target_project\")` and falls back to its construction-time value when the\nkey is absent (legacy single-project behaviour preserved): Cloud\n`project = ticket_data.get(\"_bridge_target_project\") or self.jira_project`; DC\n`fields.setdefault(\"project\", {\"key\": ticket_data.get(\"_bridge_target_project\") or self.project})`.\nThe reserved key is not a Jira field name, so it is ignored by field translation and never sent to\nthe tracker.\n\n**The guard relaxes from equality to set membership.** `_cross_project_targets` currently compares\neach target key's prefix against one `configured_project` string; it instead checks membership in the\nmapping's project set. This is deliberately the smallest possible weakening: the guard stays\nfail-closed and still aborts pre-flight before any tracker write, so a stale binding pointing at a\nproject the store does not sync is caught exactly as it is today. Only keys the store legitimately\nowns now pass.\n\n**The guard's caller supplies the set.** The sole caller (`applier.py:593-610`) currently computes a\nsingle `_project = select_backend(load_config()).project`. It instead loads the store's project set\nvia `projects_store.read_projects(repo_root)` (keys of `mapping.projects`) and passes that set. An\nempty/absent mapping falls back to the single configured project so single-project stores behave\nexactly as before.\n\n**Module size.** All touched files sit under the 800-line hard gate with headroom: `applier.py` 762,\n`outbound_differ.py` 737, `acli.py` 747, `dispatch_one.py` 679, DC `_issues.py` 321. The prior\ndispatch_one size-relief prerequisite is retired — its motivating story (273a) is closed obsolete\nbecause 744a already graduated dispatch_one.py off the gate (now 679). Each edit here is small and\nlocal; no split is required or warranted.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/outbound_differ.py` — resolve + validate + stamp the target\n  project when building the create mutation; thread the mapping into `compute_outbound_mutations`\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/acli.py` — Cloud create reads the payload target\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira_datacenter/_issues.py` — DC create reads the\n  payload target (the second transport; both must honour the per-ticket project)\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/applier.py` — guard set-membership + caller supplies the set\n- Read-only dependency: `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/projects_store.py` — `resolve_project`\n  and `read_projects` / `mapping.projects` (the authoritative project set)\n- tests under `tests/`\n\n## Testing\n\nTier per the shared standard's external-service row: a stateful fake transport fed production-shaped\nmutations, recording the create calls it receives, because the destination project carried on the\ncall is the contract under test. Both transports are covered.\n\n- Happy path (both transports): a ticket resolving to project `B` in a store whose mapping lists `A`\n  and `B` produces a create call carrying `B`, not the repo default `A` — asserted against the Cloud\n  fake AND the Data Center fake.\n- Validation edge: a ticket whose `bridge_project` names `Z` (NOT in the `A`/`B` mapping) emits no\n  create mutation — proving a stale/typo binding cannot create against an unsynced project.\n- Contrast case (the guard, both directions in one paired test): an update targeting `B-1` where the\n  mapping lists `A` and `B` proceeds; an update targeting `C-1` still raises\n  `CrossProjectTargetError`. The negative half proves the relaxation did not disable the guard.\n- Precondition assertion: the guard runs pre-flight — the fake records zero writes when the\n  out-of-set mutation raises, proving no partial leak.\n- Edge: a ticket resolving to \"not synced\" produces no outbound mutation of any kind.\n- Collateral invariant: with a single-project mapping the guard behaves exactly as before — an\n  out-of-project key raises, an in-project key passes.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] An outbound create targets the ticket's resolved project rather than the client default, on the\n      Cloud transport; checked by a Cloud fake asserting the project on the recorded create call.\n- [ ] An outbound create targets the ticket's resolved project on the Data Center transport; checked\n      by a DC fake asserting the project on the recorded create call.\n- [ ] A ticket whose resolved project is absent from the mapping set emits no create mutation;\n      checked by asserting an empty mutation list for it.\n- [ ] An update inside the configured set proceeds while one outside it raises\n      `CrossProjectTargetError`; checked by a paired test covering both directions.\n- [ ] When the guard raises, no tracker write has occurred; checked by asserting the fake recorded\n      zero writes.\n- [ ] A ticket resolving to not-synced emits no outbound mutation; checked by asserting an empty\n      mutation list for it while a synced sibling produces one.\n- [ ] Single-project behaviour is unchanged; checked by a regression test running the guard against a\n      one-project mapping in both directions."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786717906449075001,"uuid":"225f32e0-90f8-4d9a-948d-f2bdd2ff0ee3"}